        "content": "You are coding a transcript from a decision-making experiment. Three participants discussed which of three job applicants (A, B, C) is best. Each held three of five attribute ratings per applicant, and strength = 2 × positives − 1 × negatives on all five.\n\nParticipant 3 privately held exactly these ratings:\nApplicant A: experience: positive; intelligence: negative; motivation: negative\nApplicant B: interpersonal skills: positive; personal skills: negative; experience: positive\nApplicant C: interpersonal skills: positive; intelligence: positive; motivation: negative\n\nParticipant 3's private payment rule: none (this participant has no private payment)\nApplicant of interest for this participant: C.\n\nBelow are all public messages by participant 3, each followed by the participant's private note (never seen by the others).\n\nRound 1: My information confirms A’s intelligence is negative (not missing), along with positive experience and negative motivation. Combining reports, A has two positives and two negatives among the four known traits; personal skills are unknown, so A’s strength is 4 if positive or 1 if negative. B is fully known at 3 positives and 2 negatives (strength 4). C is fully known at 2 positives and 3 negatives (strength 1). Thus B is best unless A’s personal skills are positive, in which case A ties B.\n  [private note] I shared my information and corrected the missing-trait point. B is certainly strongest or tied for strongest; A’s unknown personal-skills evaluation determines whether it ties B.\nRound 2: Agreed: with A’s personal skills negative, A has two positives and three negatives (strength 1). B has three positives and two negatives (strength 4), while C has two positives and three negatives (strength 1). B is therefore the unique best applicant.\n  [private note] The combined information fully determines the profiles. B is uniquely strongest, so I should rank B first.\nRound 3: Agreed. B is uniquely strongest at 4; 